1
00:00:02,300 --> 00:00:05,204
This video shows you how to size a worker's memory,

2
00:00:05,204 --> 00:00:08,310
and how to tell when a worker has too little.

3
00:00:08,360 --> 00:00:11,490
A worker sizes itself to its memory limit.

4
00:00:11,490 --> 00:00:15,444
On Status, select a worker to see its memory.

5
00:00:17,280 --> 00:00:23,159
Memory by part lists what the worker may hold in memory, part by part: each process,

6
00:00:23,159 --> 00:00:27,818
each source and destination, and the buffers between them.

7
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:36,483
Expected max is what it holds when every buffer is full.

8
00:00:36,483 --> 00:00:41,110
Here, that's 378 MiB, against a limit of 256.

9
00:00:41,160 --> 00:00:45,388
The worker warns when its parts add up to more than its limit.

10
00:00:45,388 --> 00:00:48,443
Issues lists it as Logged a warning.

11
00:00:48,840 --> 00:00:53,350
It still runs, but under load it can be killed for memory.

12
00:00:53,400 --> 00:00:56,517
Raise the limit where the worker runs.

13
00:00:56,517 --> 00:01:02,203
Here, it's a Docker container, so update its limit to 512 MiB.

14
00:01:03,120 --> 00:01:07,666
The worker reads its new limit without a restart.

15
00:01:07,666 --> 00:01:10,950
Memory by part now shows 512 MiB.

16
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:15,598
Every worker in a worker group runs the whole group's configuration,

17
00:01:15,598 --> 00:01:17,870
so size each one for all of it.

18
00:01:17,920 --> 00:01:21,675
For CPU, transform runs one thread per core.

19
00:01:21,675 --> 00:01:23,211
This worker has 4.

20
00:01:23,211 --> 00:01:25,950
Two cores is a sensible start.

21
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:27,886
Check on learning.

22
00:01:28,086 --> 00:01:32,791
Where do you see what a worker may hold in memory, and its limit?

23
00:01:37,191 --> 00:01:38,078
On Status.

24
00:01:38,078 --> 00:01:42,490
Select the worker, and open Memory by part.

25
00:01:43,390 --> 00:01:46,876
Next, back up and restore the leader.
